  faqs={[
    {q:"Why is Chitré considered good value for expats?", a:"The combination of full urban services (hospitals, supermarkets, banks, restaurants) with prices significantly lower than more-marketed destinations like Boquete or Coronado. A 2-bedroom furnished apartment rents for $600–$900/month. Golf club membership runs about $100/month. A private doctor visit is under $15. The numbers are hard to match anywhere else in Panama with this level of infrastructure."},
    {q:"What is Chitré's Carnival like?", a:"Chitré's Carnival is considered one of the best in Panama and ranks among the top in Latin America. Held in February, it transforms the city for 4 days with elaborate floats, traditional Pollera dress, live music, and street celebrations that draw tens of thousands of visitors. Residents frequently describe it as a defining part of living in Chitré."},
    {q:"Is there good healthcare in Chitré?", a:"Yes — the Chitré public hospital is the most modern and largest on the Azuero Peninsula. There are also private clinics and specialist offices in the city. For highly specialized procedures, Panama City is 4 hours away by road. Most expats in Chitré maintain a local insurance plan for routine care and budget for the occasional trip to the capital for specialties."},
    {q:"What are the beaches near Chitré like?", a:"The Azuero Peninsula has excellent Pacific beaches within 30–60 minutes of Chitré. Playa El Agallito (15 minutes) is known for shorebirds and artisanal fishing. Pedasí's beaches and Playa Venao (surf) are about 60–90 minutes south. The beaches are significantly less crowded and more authentic than those near Panama City or Coronado."},
